The Core Concept in Plain English
A karaoke player only needs to check one simple condition whenever the music time updates:
In Data: Store your lyrics inside each song as an array of objects with seconds (time) and text (text).
In HTML: Provide a single <div id="lyricsContainer"></div>.
In JS: As the song plays, find the active line whose timestamp has arrived, add the .active class to it, and scroll it smoothly into view.
Step 1: Add Timed Lyrics to your js spotipart.js
Inside your existing playlist array, add a lyrics array with timestamps in seconds:
JavaScript
const playlist = [
    { 
        id: 1, 
        title: "Title Alpha", 
        artist: "Artist Alpha", 
        album: "Album Alpha", 
        duration: "2:45",
        // ⭐ NEW: Add timestamp (in seconds) + text
        lyrics: [
            { time: 0, text: "♪ (Intro music playing...) ♪" },
            { time: 4, text: "Late night coding with the terminal glow" },
            { time: 8, text: "Drinking hot coffee and letting rhythm flow" },
            { time: 14, text: "Writing our loops and functions in line" },
            { time: 20, text: "Everything compiles and runs just fine" },
            { time: 28, text: "Spotipart player shines tonight" }
        ]
    },
    // ... other songs
];
Step 2: Add the Container to index.html
Place this inside your <main class="main-content"> (for example, below the tracks section):
Html
<!-- Dynamic Karaoke Lyrics Section -->
<section class="lyrics-section">
    <h3 style="margin-bottom: 12px;">🎤 Live Karaoke Lyrics</h3>
    <div id="lyricsContainer" class="lyrics-container">
        <p>Select a track to see lyrics...</p>
    </div>
</section>
Step 3: Add Styling to css spotipart.css
Add these few lines to make the active karaoke line glow in Spotify green and scroll smoothly:
CSS
/* Scrollable lyrics box */
.lyrics-container {
    max-height: 240px;
    overflow-y: auto;
    background: #181818;
    border: 1px solid #282828;
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 16px;
    scroll-behavior: smooth;
}

/* Default dimmed lyric line */
.lyric-line {
    padding: 8px 12px;
    font-size: 16px;
    color: #666;
    border-radius: 6px;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: all 0.2s ease;
}

/* ⭐ The active karaoke line */
.lyric-line.active {
    color: #1db954; /* Spotify green */
    font-size: 20px;
    font-weight: 800;
    background: rgba(29, 185, 84, 0.15);
    transform: scale(1.02);
}
Step 4: Add the 2 Functions in js spotipart.js
Add these two short functions and hook them into your playback:
JavaScript
const lyricsContainer = document.getElementById("lyricsContainer");

// 1. Create the lyric elements when a song starts
function renderLyrics(song) {
    lyricsContainer.innerHTML = "";
    if (!song.lyrics) return;

    song.lyrics.forEach((line) => {
        const div = document.createElement("div");
        div.className = "lyric-line";
        div.textContent = line.text;

        // Bonus: Click any lyric line to jump the song to that exact second!
        div.addEventListener("click", () => {
            audioProgress = line.time;
            updateLyrics(audioProgress);
        });

        lyricsContainer.appendChild(div);
    });
}

// 2. Synchronize the active line as time updates
function updateLyrics(currentTime) {
    const song = playlist[currentTrackIndex];
    if (!song || !song.lyrics) return;

    // Find the latest lyric line whose timestamp has arrived
    let activeIndex = -1;
    for (let i = 0; i < song.lyrics.length; i++) {
        if (currentTime >= song.lyrics[i].time) {
            activeIndex = i;
        }
    }

    // Highlight the active line and scroll it into center
    const allLines = lyricsContainer.querySelectorAll(".lyric-line");
    allLines.forEach((lineEl, idx) => {
        if (idx === activeIndex) {
            lineEl.classList.add("active");
            lineEl.scrollIntoView({ behavior: "smooth", block: "center" });
        } else {
            lineEl.classList.remove("active");
        }
    });
}
Whenever your audio time updates (inside your setInterval progress timer or audio.ontimeupdate), simply call:
JavaScript
updateLyrics(audioProgress);
And in playSongByIndex(index), call:
JavaScript
renderLyrics(playlist[index]);
